# Cloudflare Pages header table.
#
# `opengraph-image.tsx` (Next's file convention) exports a static PNG at
# build time, but the exported file is named `opengraph-image` with NO
# extension — Next only sets a Content-Type header for it when a server is
# handling the request; a static export has no server, so the host is left to
# guess the MIME type from the filename alone. An extensionless file is not a
# guessable one: left unset, Cloudflare Pages serves it as
# application/octet-stream, which several social-platform image validators
# (Twitter/X's card validator in particular) trust over content sniffing and
# will refuse to render.
#
# One line per route that has an `opengraph-image.tsx` — `*` here matches a
# single path segment (the dynamic id), never across a `/`, so each depth
# needs its own line; a trailing `/*` splat would be needed to cross segments
# and isn't used here since every one of these routes is a fixed depth.
/gpu/*/opengraph-image
  Content-Type: image/png

/zh/gpu/*/opengraph-image
  Content-Type: image/png

/quant-hub/*/opengraph-image
  Content-Type: image/png

/zh/quant-hub/*/opengraph-image
  Content-Type: image/png

/cookbook/*/opengraph-image
  Content-Type: image/png

/zh/cookbook/*/opengraph-image
  Content-Type: image/png

/best/*/opengraph-image
  Content-Type: image/png

/zh/best/*/opengraph-image
  Content-Type: image/png

/formats/*/opengraph-image
  Content-Type: image/png

/zh/formats/*/opengraph-image
  Content-Type: image/png
